     "ikon": "grafik"
    }
   ],
   "cumleler": [
    "A transient-evoked otoacoustic emission, or TEOAE, is the cochlear response to a brief stimulus.",
    "The stimulus is usually a click; because a click is very short, it stimulates a wide frequency band at once.",
    "So many regions of the cochlea respond together, and the response is recorded as a mix of different frequencies.",
    "The response begins a few milliseconds after the stimulus and is recorded within a window of about twenty milliseconds.",
    "High frequency components come from the base of the cochlea, so they arrive earlier; low frequency components arrive later.",
    "Recall from the previous lesson: the response comes from the outer hair cells, and it is recorded after passing through the middle ear."
   ]
  },
