    {
     "metin": "Emission is partly preserved",
     "cumle": 5,
     "ikon": "kulak"
    }
   ],
   "cumleler": [
    "The basic problem in TEOAE recording is that the stimulus echo in the ear canal is very large, while the emission is very small.",
    "In the nonlinear protocol, clicks are presented in groups of four: three clicks have the same polarity.",
    "The fourth click has the opposite polarity and is three times stronger than the others.",
    "When the four responses are added, the ear canal and probe echo, which follows the stimulus linearly, largely cancels out.",
    "The cochlear emission, however, does not grow linearly; as the stimulus gets stronger, the response grows less and nears saturation.",
    "So part of the emission is preserved in the sum and can be separated from the stimulus artifact.",
    "A linear protocol gives a larger response but makes the artifact hard to remove; this is why the nonlinear protocol is common in the clinic."
   ]
  },
